**The Last Signal from Platform Seven**

The station clock had stopped at 2:17, its second hand frozen mid-tick like a thought interrupted. Everyone said it was broken, but Arun knew better. Time behaved strangely in places where too many goodbyes had happened, and Platform Seven was one of those places.

He stood with his canvas backpack slung over one shoulder, fingers wrapped around a paper ticket that had softened with sweat. The train was late—again—but the station breathed as if it expected something important. Tea vendors shouted into the evening haze. A stray dog slept curled under a bench, dreaming of nothing. The sky had the bruised purple of a day that refused to end cleanly.

Arun wasn’t supposed to be here. He had promised himself he’d leave the city quietly, without ritual or farewell. Yet here he was, drawn by a message that made no sense.

*Come to Platform Seven at sunset. Bring the radio.*

The radio sat heavy in his bag, an old handheld with a cracked antenna. It hadn’t worked in years. His father had given it to him on his twelfth birthday, saying it could catch voices from places ordinary ears couldn’t reach. Arun had laughed back then, the way children laugh at myths they half-believe. After his father disappeared, the radio became an artifact—proof of a world that might still be listening.

A breeze passed, carrying the metallic smell of rails. The loudspeaker crackled to life and died again. No announcements. No apologies.

“Waiting for someone?” asked a voice beside him.

Arun turned. A woman in a station uniform stood there, her cap tilted slightly too far back. Her eyes were sharp, curious, the color of rainclouds just before a storm.

“Just a train,” Arun said.

She smiled as if he’d told a joke. “Trains don’t come to Seven anymore.”

He looked down the platform. The rails were rusty, the sleepers choked with weeds. He had noticed, but chosen not to ask.

“Then why are you here?” he asked.

She shrugged. “Someone has to keep the lights on.” She gestured to the flickering bulbs overhead. “And you?”

Arun hesitated. Explaining the message would make it real, and he wasn’t ready for that. “I’m leaving,” he said instead. “Thought I’d say goodbye to the city.”

“That’s never enough,” she said gently. “Cities take more than one goodbye.”

Before he could respond, the lights dimmed, then steadied. The woman glanced at her watch—unbroken, ticking with confident purpose.

“It’s time,” she said.

“For what?”

She nodded at his bag. “For you to listen.”

Arun swallowed and pulled out the radio. Its plastic casing was scratched, the tuning dial stiff with neglect. He turned it on. Static hissed, a sound like distant rain.

He adjusted the dial. More static. Then—faint, unmistakable—a voice.

“…if you can hear me, stay where you are.”

Arun’s breath caught. The voice was older, roughened by years, but he knew it the way you know your own name spoken in the dark.

“Dad?” he whispered.

The woman watched him carefully, as if studying a rare bird.

The radio crackled again. “I don’t have much time. Signals don’t hold here. Arun, I need you to understand something.”

Arun sank onto a bench, the cold iron seeping through his jeans. “Where are you?” he asked. “Everyone said you left. That you didn’t want to come back.”

A pause. Then a soft laugh. “I never left. Not really. I just stepped out of sync.”

Arun remembered the night his father vanished—the half-packed bag, the radio missing from the shelf, the note that said only *I’m sorry. I’ll explain.* No explanation ever came.

“You said the radio could hear impossible things,” Arun said, voice trembling. “Was that true?”

“Yes,” his father replied. “But listening has a cost.”

The station seemed to lean closer. Even the dog stirred.

“There are places between moments,” the voice continued. “Places like this platform. I found one, and I thought I could map it, control it. I was wrong. Time isn’t a road; it’s a tide. I got caught.”

Arun closed his eyes. Anger flared—hot, sharp. “You left me,” he said. “You left Mom.”

“I know,” his father said. “And I’m sorry. That’s why I called you. Someone needs to close the loop.”

The woman stepped forward. “He can’t stay,” she said to the radio. “The window’s narrowing.”

“I don’t want him to stay,” the voice replied. “I want him to choose.”

Arun looked up at the woman. “Choose what?”

She pointed down the tracks. At the far end of Platform Seven, a glow was forming—soft at first, then brighter, like dawn being unwrapped. A train emerged from the light, impossibly clean, its windows glowing with a warm, steady amber.

“It only comes once,” she said. “You can board it and go where your father went. Or you can let it pass.”

“And if I go?” Arun asked.

“You’ll find answers,” she said. “But you won’t come back the same. Maybe not at all.”

The radio crackled urgently. “Arun, listen to me. This isn’t about fixing the past. It’s about carrying the future forward. If you come, you’ll see wonders—and you’ll lose things you didn’t know you needed.”

Arun thought of his mother, alone in the apartment with the leaky tap and the photograph she never moved. He thought of the job offer waiting unanswered, the life he’d planned like a careful sketch. He thought of the radio, the promise of voices beyond the ordinary.

“What if I stay?” he asked.

The woman smiled, sad and proud at once. “Then the platform closes. The signal fades. And you live with the not-knowing.”

The train slowed, doors sighing open. Light spilled onto the platform, warming Arun’s face. Inside, he saw silhouettes—travelers with hopeful eyes, hands pressed to windows, each carrying a version of longing.

The radio spoke one last time. “Whatever you choose, know this: I’m proud of you.”

Arun stood. His heart hammered like it was trying to outrun time itself. He took one step toward the train, then stopped.

He turned the radio off.

The light dimmed. The doors slid shut. The train began to move, its glow thinning into the distance until it was just another rumor of brightness swallowed by night.

The station exhaled.

The woman tipped her cap. “That was brave,” she said.

Arun didn’t trust his voice, so he nodded. He slipped the radio back into his bag, lighter now, as if it had finally finished speaking.

The clock on the wall shuddered, then lurched forward. 2:18. The second hand ticked, steady and sure.

A whistle blew from another platform. A real train this time—late, loud, ordinary. Arun shouldered his bag and walked toward it, carrying a quiet ache and a fragile peace.

As he boarded, he glanced back at Platform Seven. The lights flickered once, then went dark. For a moment, he thought he heard static—just a whisper—but it might have been the wind.

The train pulled away, and the city opened before him, vast and unfinished. Time moved on, imperfect and precious, and Arun moved with it, listening carefully to the silence that followed.

